From: OddBlueDog <3959851+OddBlueDog@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] functions: an RPS-spread Ethernet port's ifb gets a queue per CPU too

109 gave a PPP device's ifb a queue per CPU because the kernel picks an
ifb's queue by the receive queue a packet came in on, and a PPPoE
session's packets all arrive on its port's first queue. An Ethernet port
whose packets RPS spreads is the same: with packet steering's RSS off
(milkflint.steering.rss=0) or OpenWrt's layout, the port's packets all
come in on its first ring, and with cake_mq on the download every CPU RPS
hands them to fed sub-queue 1, its lock shared by three CPUs, the other
sub-queues idle.

Such a port's ifb now gets a queue per CPU too: when the mask of the
port's first receive queue isn't empty, or of the port under a device on
top of one (a VLAN WAN: RPS runs on the port). With RSS the mask is empty
and the kernel's choice by ring stays. Packet steering off still gets
nothing.

--- a/src/functions.sh
+++ b/src/functions.sh
@@ -287,9 +287,13 @@
 # That needs each flow to stay on one CPU: not with packet steering off,
 # whose threads move between CPUs (packet-steering.sh also puts this right
 # for the ifbs already there when steering is switched).
+# An Ethernet port's too, when RPS spreads what its first receive queue
+# takes (packet steering without the hardware's RSS, or OpenWrt's layout):
+# its packets all came in on that queue, as a PPPoE session's do; and a
+# device's on top of such a port (a VLAN), as RPS runs on the port.
 ifb_queue_per_cpu() {
     local q
-    [ "$(cat /sys/class/net/$1/type 2> /dev/null)" = 512 ] || return 0
+    [ "$(cat /sys/class/net/$1/type 2> /dev/null)" = 512 ] || rps_spreads $1 || return 0
     [ "$(uci -q get network.@globals[0].packet_steering)" != 0 ] || return 0
     # one queue (multi-queue off): nothing to choose
     [ -d /sys/class/net/$2/queues/tx-1 ] || return 0
@@ -300,6 +304,25 @@
     done
 }
 
+# MilkFlint: whether RPS spreads what device $1 receives over the CPUs: its
+# first receive queue's mask isn't empty, or, for a device on top of one
+# other (a VLAN, a DSA port), that one's down to the port, where RPS runs
+rps_spreads() {
+    local dev=$1 m lower i=0
+    while [ $i -lt 4 ]; do
+        m=$(cat /sys/class/net/$dev/queues/rx-0/rps_cpus 2> /dev/null)
+        case "$m" in
+            ''|*[!0-9a-fA-F,]*) ;;
+            *) [ -n "$(echo "$m" | tr -d '0,')" ] && return 0 ;;
+        esac
+        lower=$(ls -d /sys/class/net/$dev/lower_* 2> /dev/null)
+        [ -n "$lower" ] && [ "$(echo "$lower" | wc -l)" -eq 1 ] || return 1
+        dev=${lower##*/lower_}
+        i=$((i + 1))
+    done
+    return 1
+}
+
 
